WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.684 --> 00:00:03.267
(gentle music)

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:08.860
- [Pardis] Early on when my
research wasn't going that well,

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:10.900
and I was having trouble,
people would be like,

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:12.760
well, she's in a band.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:14.780
But then when my research
started going well,

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:17.020
and I started publishing,
they'd be like wow,

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:18.123
and she's in a band.

00:00:19.266 --> 00:00:20.967
- [Bob] One, two, three, four

00:00:20.967 --> 00:00:24.946
♪ Count me out ♪
♪ Don't count me out ♪

00:00:24.946 --> 00:00:28.895
♪ Count me out ♪
♪ Don't count me out ♪

00:00:28.895 --> 00:00:31.767
♪ Count me out ♪

00:00:31.767 --> 00:00:33.010
- [Pardis] People think that
you have to be one-dimensional

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:35.530
to be a scientist, but actually
your science gets better

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:37.060
when you stimulate your creativity,

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:38.850
your curiosity, all of that.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:41.220
So to me actually, doing music

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:42.968
helps me be a better scientist.

00:00:42.968 --> 00:00:46.364
♪ Count me out ♪
♪ Don't count me out ♪

00:00:46.364 --> 00:00:48.040
I'm Pardis Sabeti, I'm a geneticist

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:51.240
and I'm a National
Geographic Emerging Explorer.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.633
I use math to dissect
the genomes of viruses,

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:57.200
to decode them, and understand

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:58.800
how they evolve, how they spread

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:02.670
and ultimately how to
prevent infectious disease

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:03.823
and prevent outbreaks.

00:01:05.986 --> 00:01:07.570
I was born in Tehran, Iran.

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:09.260
I came here when I was just a little girl.

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:10.970
And the middle school me

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.494
was just a little girl who loved math.

00:01:13.494 --> 00:01:16.585
So much, I just loved the
logic and the puzzles,

00:01:16.585 --> 00:01:20.473
and just as soon as I really
got into it, I never stopped.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:26.620
Math is a powerful tool to
mine vast amounts of data,

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:29.890
genomics is just thousands,
millions, billions of letters

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:32.870
that you're trying to decode, decipher,

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:34.350
using math, using computers.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:36.373
That's what I do and that's what I love.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:42.000
So a virus is a microscopic
thing that can infect us

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.000
and can cause a lot of
harm, can make us very sick.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.740
And I study all sorts of viruses,

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.703
from the common flu to Ebola.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:54.390
Ebola is a very deadly virus.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:56.320
It can have really high mortality rates,

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.346
and it can spread really quickly.

00:01:58.346 --> 00:02:01.140
(soft music)

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:04.280
In kind of these worst case
outbreaks it can be 80%,

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:06.333
which is why we're
really worried about it.

00:02:08.230 --> 00:02:09.930
An outbreak is an infectious disease

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:11.100
that's gotten out of control.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:12.680
You can think of it like a wildfire.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.220
You never really know
where one will start,

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:19.010
but you need to detect it
and you need to contain it

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:20.630
before it gets out of control.

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:21.810
That's what my work does

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:24.403
for infectious disease, for outbreaks.

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:30.650
During the 2014 outbreak
of Ebola in West Africa,

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:34.190
we essentially, when a patient's infected,

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:36.890
their blood sample is taken for diagnosis.

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:38.300
We're able to take that sample,

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:40.680
deactivate the virus within it

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:44.530
and ship it to the United States

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:46.680
where we were able to prepare them

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.320
and put them on sequencing machines

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:49.784
and read out their genomes.

00:02:49.784 --> 00:02:52.201
(soft music)

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:55.460
So once we sequence a virus' genome,

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:56.690
it might look something like this.

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:58.690
This is actually Ebola's genome sequence

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:00.500
and there's clues in here.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:02.690
And we can use math and computation

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:04.643
to start to decipher those clues.

00:03:04.643 --> 00:03:08.763
Learn about the virus'
history, and how it's changing.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.410
All of this is arming us
towards better detection,

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:15.290
surveillance, prevention.

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:17.664
Really to be able to
put out that wildfire.

00:03:17.664 --> 00:03:19.260
(upbeat music)

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:21.500
As somebody who studies
infectious diseases,

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:24.156
we're most successful when
nothing happens, right?

00:03:24.156 --> 00:03:27.610
We are trying to stop
outbreaks from ever occurring.

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:29.650
And so we're most
successful at that point,

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:32.550
when we put out the sparks
and you never hear about it.

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:34.813
But that's sort of an exciting challenge.

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:41.830
So one of the tools we created
is an outbreak simulation

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:45.930
that's spread over Bluetooth
from phone to phone

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:48.130
for middle schoolers, to get to experience

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:50.070
what an outbreak might be like,

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:51.763
but before a real life event.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.850
So you know we created this
tool to be able to educate

00:03:55.850 --> 00:03:57.770
and have outreach to students,

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:01.920
but really we learned as much
from them as anything else.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:03.940
Their participation taught us

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:06.340
about how people respond to outbreaks.

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:08.970
Their ingenuity, their thought process

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:10.870
during the outbreak taught us so much.

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:16.700
I love working in outbreaks
because it requires cooperation,

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:19.750
it requires people working
together, collaborating

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:21.580
and that's how you win.

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:23.880
It's sort of the way I like
to think about the world.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:27.810
I mean I'm in a band, I'm
not a singer, songwriter,

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.750
I like being part of a collective.

00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:32.110
It's where the best things come together.

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:34.979
(guitar strumming)
♪ When I breathe out ♪

00:04:34.979 --> 00:04:38.062
♪ I breathe out love ♪

00:04:39.476 --> 00:04:42.309
- [Pardis] All right, there we go.

